         <p>The App stores learning progress, streaks, achievements, flashcards, daily tasks, lesson state, settings, notification settings, cached activity/error queues, cached assets, and similar app data on your device using AsyncStorage, SecureStore where available, and related platform storage. Devices that used earlier versions may still hold legacy local state created by features that have since been retired or rebuilt (such as the old Quiz mode or earlier versions of competitive modes). That state does not mean those features remain available in that form and is removed through the ordinary local-data or account-deletion controls described below. After account deletion, the App also clears the offline cache kept by the Firebase database library the next time it starts.</p>
         <p>The App also creates or uses a Phraseman stable app identifier. It may be stored in SecureStore where available and mirrored in local storage so progress, cloud sync, purchases, referrals, moderation, fraud prevention, account recovery, and account deletion can work across app sessions. Depending on iOS Keychain, Android backup/restore, device restore, or device settings, this identifier or a backup of it may survive reinstall or device restore until you use the in-app deletion flow, clear app data, or reset relevant device storage.</p>
         <p>If you use the App as a guest (without signing in with Google or Apple), your progress is tied to this installation. Deleting the app, clearing its data, or moving to a new device may permanently lose it. Sign in with Google or Apple to keep it.</p>
+        <p>Home-screen quick actions (the shortcuts shown when you press and hold the app icon, for example to continue your last lesson) are created on your device from your local app state; they do not send any information to us.</p>
         <p>When you switch accounts on a device, the App may keep a temporary copy of the previous account's local progress on that device, so that it can be returned if you sign back in to the same account. This copy stays on the device, is only restored into the same account, and is discarded after 30 days.</p>
 
         <h2 id="s5-4-account-authentication-and-cloud-sync">4. Account, authentication, and cloud sync</h2>
         <p>The App uses Firebase Authentication. By default, Firebase may create an anonymous app session so cloud features can operate without a separate Knowly username/password.</p>
         <p>You may optionally sign in with Google or Apple. If you do, Firebase and Knowly may process provider identifiers, Firebase Auth UID, email address if shared, display name if shared, provider type, platform, sign-in timestamps, account-linking records, and related authentication metadata. We use this to restore progress, link devices, prevent account drift, secure the service, and prevent abuse.</p>
-        <p>Cloud sync may store progress and app state in Firebase Firestore, including lessons, streaks, achievements, certificates, custom flashcards, your &quot;My mistakes&quot; history (the exercise, the correct answer and the answer you gave, up to 200 characters), videos you save to your favourites (the video ID and the time you saved it), daily and weekly stats, foreground usage totals, Plus status metadata, RevenueCat purchase metadata, coin and energy balances and ledgers, Peak progress, settings, language/target selections, referral state, AI dialogue memory (described in the AI features section), social feature state, and other data needed to operate requested features.</p>
+        <p>Cloud sync may store progress and app state in Firebase Firestore, including lessons, streaks, achievements, certificates, custom flashcards, your &quot;My mistakes&quot; history (the exercise, the correct answer and the answer you gave, up to 200 characters), videos you save to your favourites (the video ID and the time you saved it), daily and weekly stats, foreground usage totals, Plus status metadata, RevenueCat purchase metadata, coin and energy balances and ledgers, Peak progress, settings, language/target selections, referral state, AI dialogue memory (described in the AI features section), social feature state, and other data needed to operate requested features. This includes your progress in each course language you study (for example Spanish, French, German or Italian courses), which is stored under your account so it follows you to a new device.</p>
         <p>If you lose access to your progress, for example after reinstalling the App or choosing a different Google or Apple account, the App may offer to restore it with a one-time code. The code is sent by email, through our email provider (Resend), only to the email address already linked to your account. It expires after 10 minutes. We store only a protected (hashed) form of the code, together with attempt counters and recovery records used to prevent abuse.</p>
         <p>To protect account recovery against code guessing and other abuse, the App keeps short-lived counters of recovery requests and incorrect codes. Each counter is identified only by a keyed hash (made with a secret key) of your sign-in account identifier, of the account being recovered or the email address entered for recovery, or of the IP address the request came from; we do not store the IP address itself in these counters. A counter contains only a number and timestamps, is used only to limit repeated attempts during its limit period (at most one day), and is otherwise retained only as described in the &quot;Retention&quot; section below.</p>
         <p>To protect the service and its running costs against abuse, the App also keeps short-lived counters for nickname changes, nickname availability checks, and requests to features that call paid AI services (such as dialogue practice, conversation review, translation and voice). Each counter is identified only by a one-way hash of your account identifier or of the IP address the request came from; we do not store the IP address itself in these counters. A counter contains only a number and timestamps, is used only to limit repeated requests during its limit period (one hour or one day), and is otherwise retained only as described in the &quot;Retention&quot; section below.</p>
-        <p>If you save a video to your favourites, we store the video ID and the time you saved it in your account so your list follows you to a new device, and we keep an aggregate count of how many accounts saved each video. The count does not show who saved a video. Your list is deleted together with your account.</p>
+        <p>If you save a video to your favourites, we store the video ID and the time you saved it in your account so your list follows you to a new device, and we keep an aggregate count of how many accounts saved each video. The count does not show who saved a video. When you watch a video in the in-app catalogue through to the end (at least 70% of its length), the App grants a one-time coin reward for that video. To make the reward work across devices and to prevent it from being granted twice, we store in your account the ID of the video and the time the reward was granted; this record is used only to apply the reward once and to detect abuse, is not shown to other users, and is deleted together with your account. Your list is deleted together with your account.</p>
 
         <h2 id="s6-5-public-social-and-historical-competiti">5. Public, social, and historical competitive data</h2>
         <p>Some data is visible to other users when you use current public or social features. This may include nickname, coin-rank avatar, profile card settings and style, the short profile-card caption you write yourself (available with the Pro profile card, a card style you can get with coins; it is not a subscription), streak, rank, Peak floor, steps and weekly result, weekly coins earned (shown to members of your Peak room), historical Arena and tournament results from earlier app versions, the Plus marker described below, friend activity, activity likes, community pack author information, seller notifications, and similar profile fields. Your profile-card caption is visible to other players who open your profile card; other players can report it, and we may remove captions that break our rules. If you answer a poll in the App, we record only which questions your account has already answered, so that you cannot vote twice; the option you choose is added only to aggregated counts and is not stored with your account ID. Your own answers may be kept on your device. Other users only see aggregated results. Visibility depends on the feature: some fields may be public to app users, some visible to friends, some visible to community pack buyers or sellers, and some visible only to moderators/admins.</p>
         <p>Peak floor records. In a Peak room, the fastest accepted time for each floor is shown to the members of that room together with the name of the player who set it (a player without a visible name appears as &quot;Player&quot;). Your own best times are shown only to you. To check a record attempt, our server keeps the attempt details (a one-time token, the questions issued for the attempt and the start time) and the accepted result (the time taken, when it was completed, and confirmation that all ten answers were correct). This competitive data is separate from your lessons, coins and energy, has no separate automatic expiry, and is deleted together with your account.</p>
-        <p>If you have Phraseman Plus, other users may see a Plus marker (for example, a ring around your avatar) next to your nickname in friends lists, leaderboards, Peak rooms, and community features. The marker shows only that Plus is currently active; it does not show your plan, price, renewal date, how you got Plus, or any payment details.</p>
+        <p>If you have Phraseman Plus or Ultra, other users may see a subscription marker (for example, a ring around your avatar) next to your nickname in friends lists, leaderboards, Peak rooms, and community features. The marker's colour and shape show which plan you have: Plus (monthly subscription) or Ultra (yearly subscription); to show it we store your plan level (plus or ultra) in your public profile together with the Plus end date. Both plans give the same access. The marker shows only that Plus is currently active; it does not show your plan, price, renewal date, how you got Plus, or any payment details.</p>
         <p>Peak groups players into weekly rooms of up to 30 places for each study language. Other app users who open the room can see your nickname, avatar and cosmetic style, your steps, place and weekly result. Some places in a room may be filled by computer-controlled participants rather than real people. Weekly room records are kept as part of the Peak history; your entry is removed from them when your account is deleted.</p>
         <p>When you study with friends in the Friends section, those friends may see whether you studied today, which days of the current week you studied, how many days you both studied on the same day, your current and longest shared streak, and the resulting friendship level and progress. Friends may also send or receive nudges through this feature.</p>
         <p>Only friends you have accepted (not other users) may also see: in the weekly friend summary, how many study sessions you completed that week, your best day, and your Peak floor; in the shared phrase of the day, the phrase option you chose; in Learn together and duels, that you invited them and the result of a duel between the two of you; and, when you send a friend an energy gift, that it came from you.</p>
