         <h2 id="s7-6-user-content-community-packs-reports-a">6. User content, community packs, reports, and moderation</h2>
         <p>If you create Community Packs, submit flashcard text, translations, reports, profile names, survey answers, ratings/votes, or other user-provided content, we process the content you provide and related metadata. This may include titles, descriptions, card text and translations, normalised text used for moderation, report reasons, reporter/reported user IDs and names, author IDs, buyer/seller IDs, moderation decisions, timestamps, platform, app version, audit fields, ranking scores, vote counts, report counts, and related safety signals.</p>
         <p>Published Community Packs and approved catalogue metadata may be visible in the App. Pack cards may be visible to the author, moderators, and users who access or purchase the pack with coins.</p>
-        <p>If you comment on a Community Pack you have added, we store your comment text, your author ID and display name, the pack ID, timestamps, reaction counts, and pin status. Comments are visible to other users who have added the same pack, remain attached to the pack, and are not private messages. The pack's author can pin one comment and hide a comment from the pack's comment list for other viewers; a hidden comment is not deleted and may still be reviewed if reported. You can delete your own comment at any time, and any user can report a specific comment (separately from reporting the whole pack) using the same report categories described above. If someone comments on a Community Pack you authored, we send you an in-app notification containing a short excerpt of the comment so you can review it.</p>
+        <p>If you comment on a Community Pack you have added, we store your comment text, your author ID and display name, the pack ID, timestamps, reaction counts, and pin status. Comments are not private messages. Any signed-in user who opens the pack can see the comments on it, together with the commenter's display name. Only a user who has added the pack (or its author) can write a comment. Comments remain attached to the pack until you delete them. When you delete your account, comments you wrote under other users' packs are deleted with it; you can also delete them yourself at any time. The pack's author can pin one comment and hide a comment from the pack's comment list for other viewers; a hidden comment is not deleted and may still be reviewed if reported. You can delete your own comment at any time, and any user can report a specific comment (separately from reporting the whole pack) using the same report categories described above. If someone comments on a Community Pack you authored, we send you an in-app notification containing a short excerpt of the comment so you can review it.</p>
+        <p>Likes and additions. When you like a Community Pack or add it to your collection, we record a small marker in the pack linking your account ID to that pack, with the time. We use it so that each person can like or add a pack only once and to keep the like and add counts accurate. Other users see only the totals in the App (for example the number of likes next to a pack), not who liked or added it. You cannot like a pack you wrote. These markers are deleted when your account deletion runs. The like and add totals shown next to a pack are not recalculated afterwards, so a total may still include a like or addition from a deleted account.</p>
         <p>A Community Pack's author can edit the pack's title, description, and cards after it is published. Edited content replaces the previous version immediately and does not go through a separate moderation review before becoming visible to other users; we keep the previous version and edit metadata for audit and abuse-investigation purposes.</p>
         <p>To check Community Packs for mistakes and to show them in other interface languages, the text of a pack (title, description, and card text) may be sent to our AI provider (OpenAI) for review and automatic translation. Translated versions may be shown to other users who view the pack. When we reply to a report you sent, a moderator may use AI assistance to draft the reply from the report text; a person reviews the reply before it is sent to you in the App.</p>
         <p>When a Community Pack is approved for publication, the text of its cards in the language being learned (up to 300 characters per card) may be sent from our servers to our AI provider (OpenAI) to generate spoken audio for the cards. Only the card text is sent; no account details, names, or identifiers are sent with it. The generated audio files are stored in our cloud storage (Google Firebase) and played to users who open the pack. Because a clip depends only on its text, the same clip may be reused for identical card text in other packs. Audio is kept for as long as it is needed to provide the packs that use it and may be removed at any time.</p>
